                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_triangulate:   `<strong>Good — you diagnosed before you designed.</strong> Triangulating the CEO, CHRO and line-director views into one prioritised challenge is exactly the co-creation discipline. Now the journey can be built backward from a real outcome instead of forward from a guess.`,
                    coach_r1_ceo:           `<strong>Taking the CEO at face value is tempting, but partial.</strong> They signed the cheque, but the CHRO's retention fear and the line directors' delivery problem are real too. Design only for strategic alignment and two of your three sponsors quietly disown the programme.`,
                    coach_r1_design:        `<strong>You've sketched a leadership course in twelve minutes — impressive, and premature.</strong> The CEO means alignment, the CHRO means retention, the line directors mean delivery. You've designed for none of them confidently. If you build the wrong programme on £680k, what does cohort-1 feedback look like — and do cohorts 2 and 3 ever happen?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You triangulated the conflicting sponsors AND named capabilities tied to the restructuring outcome and the success measure. That is backward design — start from the result you'll be held to, then build the journey that produces it.`,
                    coach_r1_generic:       `<strong>Generic competencies feel safe and aim at nothing.</strong> "Broad leadership" can't be aligned to Helvern's restructuring, and it can't anchor a £68k impact measure. Name the three or four capabilities the real challenge actually demands.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>Content-first is the classic over-design trap.</strong> Assemble strong material and hope relevance emerges, and you'll build a beautiful course for a problem nobody has. Start from the outcome, not the catalogue.`,
                    coach_r1_norecontract:  `<strong>Quoting a design off a one-paragraph brief is how you mis-sell.</strong> Without a re-contracting call you commit £680k of design to a request you never tested. Thirty minutes with the sponsor now saves a wasted engagement later.`,
                    coach_r2_seventy:       `<strong>70-20-10 is the right spine.</strong> Capability is built mostly through workplace application and social learning, not teaching days. Embedding an action-learning project and coaching is what will actually shift behaviour at Helvern — and what the £68k fee depends on.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A case-and-simulation mix is solid but classroom-bound.</strong> It builds knowledge well, yet without workplace application and coaching the behaviour change stays fragile. Push more of the journey into the 70 and the 20.`,
                    coach_r2_teaching:      `<strong>Teaching days alone are the 10 of 70-20-10.</strong> "The content is what they paid for" is exactly the catalogue thinking custom design exists to replace. Teaching days inform; application and coaching change behaviour. You're under-powering the impact you'll be measured on.`,
                    coach_r2_starheavy:     `<strong>An all-star faculty is relevant but ruinous.</strong> At £4,500 a day the star practitioners are scarce and expensive — load up on them and you blow past £7,556 per participant before you've added a venue. Use a few stars as anchors, not the whole faculty.`,
                    coach_r2_allcore:       `<strong>An all-core faculty is affordable but under-powered.</strong> Solid, available people get you a competent programme, but the sponsor bought relevance. A couple of scarce star-practitioner days anchor credibility the core team can't.`,
                    coach_r2_residential:   `<strong>Full residential is immersive — and budget-hungry.</strong> Five off-site days for every cohort is £260k of format alone. Lovely if you can afford it; check it still lands under £680k once faculty and the build are in.`,
                    coach_r2_virtual:       `<strong>Virtual-only is cheap and easy to schedule — and weak on application.</strong> 90 senior managers learning leadership entirely on screen rarely changes behaviour back at the desk. You've protected the budget and thinned the impact.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Your design is beautiful — and it costs more than £680k.</strong> That's above £7,556 per participant, and procurement has a quote at £5,500. Which tens of thousands are you cutting — and did you choose them, or will the client choose them for you?`,
                    coach_r3_defend:        `<strong>Defending the value is the right move.</strong> Translate the price into business language — what the action-learning and coaching actually produce for the restructuring. A premium you can justify protects both the margin and the relationship.`,
                    coach_r3_cave:          `<strong>You just matched £5,500 to keep procurement happy.</strong> That's roughly £185,000 out of a £680k design — and you took it out of the action-learning and coaching, the only parts that produce the behaviour change your £68k success fee depends on. You protected the price and forfeited the impact. Was that the trade you meant to make?`,
                    coach_r3_rigid:         `<strong>Holding the price and refusing to engage isn't defending value — it's stonewalling.</strong> Procurement needs to understand the trade-offs, not be told no. Refuse the conversation and you lose the room, and possibly the contract.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>That's a defensible custom proposal.</strong> You held the value in business language AND tied the success fee to a Behaviour/Results measure. The premium now buys something procurement can see — and the £68k is anchored to real change, not a satisfaction score.`,
                    coach_r3_renegotiate:   `<strong>Renegotiating the fee to satisfaction feels safer and quietly hollows the promise.</strong> A reaction-level measure is easy to hit and proves nothing about behaviour. You'll likely collect the £68k and still can't tell the CEO whether 90 managers led the restructuring any better.`,
                    coach_r3_dropfee:       `<strong>Dropping the success fee removes your accountability — and your differentiator.</strong> The fee is what makes a custom design measurable. Walk away from it and you're selling teaching days again, priced like a premium.`,
                    coach_r3_overdescope:   `<strong>That's a deep de-scope.</strong> Conceding half the design value to procurement signals the original price was padded and strips out the components that produce impact. A small, chosen cut defends the design; a large one dismantles it.`,
                    coach_r4_rebrief:       `<strong>You treated the drop-out as a quality decision, not a scheduling fix.</strong> A relevance-matched, properly briefed substitute and an honest word to the sponsor protects the product they bought. Resourcing is part of the design, not just logistics.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>You swapped in whoever was free and said nothing.</strong> You saved the date and quietly downgraded the product — the original faculty member was the relevance anchor the sponsor bought, and the substitute scores well below on fit. How do you tell the client, or do you let them find out in the room?`,
                    coach_r4_delay:         `<strong>Delaying to keep the original faculty misses the 10-week launch.</strong> A flagship reference client now has a broken first promise, and Helvern can move on cohorts 2–3. Did the deadline serve the learning, or did you just break both?`,
                    coach_r4_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid behavioural-plus-KPI measure earns the fee.</strong> Pre/post behavioural ratings tied to a restructuring business KPI is exactly the Behaviour/Results evidence the £68k was promised on — and the answer to "did 90 managers actually lead the restructuring better?"`,
                    coach_r4_happy:         `<strong>Happy sheets forfeit the £68k.</strong> Cohort 1 rates you 4.6 out of 5 and the CEO asks: did 90 managers actually lead the restructuring better? You can't answer, so the success fee is gone and the renewal conversation is awkward. What would you have had to design on day one to answer that?`,
                    coach_r4_kpionly:       `<strong>A single hard KPI is bold but brittle.</strong> A restructuring outcome is shaped by far more than your programme; pin the whole fee to one business number and a confound can sink a genuinely good design. Pair it with behavioural evidence you can actually attribute.`,
                    coach_r4_repeat:        `<strong>Repeating cohort 1 identically ignores what you just learned.</strong> Cohort-1 evidence is the whole point of running it first. Name the adjustments and cohorts 2–3 get better; freeze the design and you bank the same gaps twice more.`,
                    coach_r4_pause:         `<strong>Pausing cohorts 2–3 protects you and unsettles the client.</strong> Waiting for complete data is cautious, but it stalls £453,000 of committed work and reads as a loss of nerve. Adjust and proceed if the cohort-1 signal is sound.`,
                    coach_r4_lowquality:    `<strong>Letting quality slide with the attendance is how the reference client sours.</strong> Restructuring firefighting will always compete for attention; protect the learning conditions — catch-up paths, protected coaching — or the impact you measure won't be there to find.`,

